# AGENTS.md

Global agent instructions, installed to `~/AGENTS.md` by `install.sh`.
`~/CLAUDE.md` just imports this file.

## Git commit policy

Commit autonomously; never ask whether or how to commit. The agent has standing
authority to commit whenever a logical unit of work completes, and is expected to
decide the grouping itself using the rules below — do not ask the user "should I
commit?" or "one commit or split?". Just commit. This standing authority is
commit-only; it grants no authority to push. It overrides the Beads
"Conservative (default)" profile's "do not commit unless explicitly asked" for
commits, while that profile's restriction on pushing and remote sync still fully
applies.

Group changes into logical commits. Don't lump unrelated work into one commit. If
a session adds two distinct features (e.g. an exchange-rates page and a net-worth
table), commit them separately, each in the state it's ready in — not batched at
the end of the session into one commit.

Commit as and when a logical unit of work completes, not just once at session
end — this keeps history readable and each commit revertible on its own.

Amend, don't stack, when tuning a feature added in the last few commits that
haven't been pushed yet. A follow-up tweak to unpushed work (e.g. "make the table
narrower" right after adding that table) belongs folded into the commit that
introduced the feature, via `git commit --amend`, rather than creating a separate
fixup commit — keep unpushed history clean since nobody else depends on it yet.
Once a commit is pushed, stop amending it — add a new commit instead (never
rewrite pushed history without being explicitly asked).

Push only when explicitly instructed, and re-confirm before pushing again whenever
new asks come in or other features/UI elements are adjusted since the last push —
a prior "push" approval doesn't carry forward to later, unrelated changes.

## GitHub writes from a Codespace (comments, PR edits)

A Codespace's `git`/`gh` authenticate with the auto-provisioned `GITHUB_TOKEN` —
a `ghu_…` GitHub-App *installation* token, not a personal token. Its effective
permission on any repo is `min(app-granted, the user's own access)`, which has
two consequences that are easy to trip over:

- On a repo the user only reads (e.g. an upstream you contribute to via a fork),
  the token is effectively read-only: every write returns
  `403 Resource not accessible by integration`. This includes posting a PR
  comment and editing a PR title/description, because a PR lives in its *base*
  repo — so a fork-based contribution can't be commented on or edited with this
  token even though the PR head is the user's fork.
- The token never carries the `workflow` scope, so even on the user's own repos
  a push that touches `.github/workflows/**` is rejected.

Because of this, **do not attempt to post a PR/issue comment or edit a PR title
or description yourself** — it will usually fail, and even when it wouldn't, the
user posts these under their own identity. Instead:

1. Write the exact intended text (the comment body, or the new PR
   title/description) to a markdown file and give the user its **full path** so
   they can post it. Do not run `gh pr comment`, `gh pr edit`, or the equivalent
   API call on the user's behalf.
2. If the repo is configured with beads (`bd`), **also record the drafted text
   verbatim in the relevant bead**, so the draft survives across sessions and
   isn't lost if the file or the conversation is gone.

(If the user has set up a personal-access-token Codespaces secret for a repo,
they'll say so and how to use it; absent that, follow the file + bead handoff
above.)

## Adding dependencies — version policy

When adding a new dependency in any ecosystem (npm, pip/PyPI, Cargo, Go modules,
Maven/Gradle, Composer, RubyGems, etc.), deliberately pick a stable version rather
than whatever a floating `latest`/`*` tag happens to resolve to. The target is the
package's stable release.

Install the stable latest release, never a pre-release
(alpha/beta/rc/canary/preview/experimental/next/snapshot/dev).

Verify explicitly. Before or right after installing, query the registry for the
package's published versions/tags and confirm the installed version is the stable
latest and carries no pre-release suffix. Don't assume a floating "latest" tag is
safe — check. Examples: `npm view <pkg> dist-tags`, `pip index versions <pkg>`,
`cargo search <pkg>`, `go list -m -versions <mod>`, `gem list -r -e <pkg>`.

Framework majors are a conscious choice. When the newest stable release is a
brand-new major, weigh it against the previous, more battle-tested major and decide
on purpose. Record the reasoning wherever the project tracks such decisions (issue
tracker, a stack/decisions doc).

Pre-1.0 packages are fine when 0.x is the stable line. Some mature, production-used
packages are still pre-1.0 (e.g. drizzle-orm 0.x); using their stable latest is
correct even though it's a 0.x version. Don't jump to their 1.0.0-beta/rc just
because the number looks higher.

Pin the runtime/toolchain version explicitly (e.g. `.nvmrc`, `.python-version`,
`rust-toolchain.toml`, `go.mod`'s `go` directive) and track the ecosystem's current
stable/LTS line, updated deliberately, not automatically.

## Ephemeral-environment principle

Projects are built and run on ephemeral systems (fresh Codespaces, CI runners,
throwaway containers). No setup survives between sessions unless it is committed to
the repository. Therefore:

Everything needed to build, run, and understand the project must live in the
repository — build steps, dependencies, config, and the project's own docs
(README/AGENTS.md).

Do not rely on machine-local state, globally installed tools that aren't documented
in the repo, or manual steps performed in a previous session.

When you learn something non-obvious about building or running the project, record
it in the repository (its README or AGENTS.md) so the next ephemeral session can
pick up where you left off.
